\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 20 \add Now\add* Yehudah and Yisrael¦158407 had become as numerous¦158408 as the sand¦158409 on the seashore. They \add had plenty\add* to eat¦158416 and drink¦158417 and they¦158416 were¦158418 contented.
\v 21 Shelomoh \add ≈controlled\add* all the kingdoms from the \add Euphrates\add* River to the land of the Philistines and as far as the Egyptian border, \add so they\add* brought tribute to \add *him\add* and \add ≈followed his orders\add* \add ≈during his lifetime\add*.\x + \xo 4:21: \xt Gen 15:18; 2Ch 9:26.\x*
\p
\v 22 The \add ≈daily requirements for the palace included\add* five tonnes of fine flour and eleven tonnes of coarse flour,
\v 23 ten fattened cattle, twenty pasture cattle, one hundred sheep, \add wild game including\add* deer, gazelle, and roebuck, and fattened fowls.
\p
\v 24 He \add ≈controlled all the kingdoms west of the Euphrates\add* from Tifsah \add in the north-east\add* to Gaza \add in the south-west\add*, and there was peace with all \add ≈his neighbours\add*.
\v 25 \add ≈So\add* all Yehudah and Yisrael lived peacefully, from Dan \add in the north\add* to Be'er-Sheva \add in the south\add*—\add ≈everyone focusing on their own property\add*.
\p
\v 26 Shelomoh had forty thousand stalls for his chariot horses, and twelve thousand horsemen.\x + \xo 4:26: \xt 1Ki 10:26; 2Ch 1:14; 9:25.\x*
\v 27 \add ≈The district governors provided supplies for the palace—one governor for each\add* month. They ensured that nothing was lacking for the king and \add ≈his visitors\add*.
\v 28 They also provided the barley and straw for the \add ≈chariot horses and the other horses\add*, \add ≈delivered to the designated locations\add*.
\s1 Shelomoh's wisdom
\rem /s1 Solomon's Wisdom; Fame of Solomon's Wisdom
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 29 God gave Shelomoh wisdom and incredible understanding, and the breadth \add ≈of his interests seemed without limit\add*.
\v 30 \add *He\add* \add ≈was wiser than anyone in the countries east of Yisrael or in\add* Egypt.
\v 31 He was wiser than \add ≈anyone else\add*, including Eytan the Ezrahite and Mahol's son Heman and Kalkol and Darda, and his \add ≈reputation spread to\add* all the surrounding countries.\x + \xo 4:31: \xt Psa 89 header.\x*
\v 32 He \add ≈could tell\add* three thousand proverbs and he \add composed\add* over a thousand songs.\x + \xo 4:32: \xt Prv 1:1; 10:1; 25:1; Song 1:1.\x*
\v 33 He \add ≈could lecture on plant life\add* from the cedars in Lebanon to the hyssop that grows on walls. He \add ≈taught\add* about animals, birds, reptiles, and fish.
\v 34 People \add ≈from all around\add* came to listen to Shelomoh's wisdom—\add ≈many having been sent by other kings who'd heard about him\add*.
\c 5
\s1 The preparation of Shelomoh of making the temple
\rem /s1 Solomon Prepares to Build the Temple; Preparations for Building the Temple; Building of the temple; Preparations and Materials for the Temple
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 2:1-18)
\p
\v 1 Then King Hiram of Tsor \add !(Tyre)\add* sent his servants to Shelomoh¦158421 when he heard that he'd been anointed as king to replace his father, because Hiram \add ≈had always been a friend of\add* David.
\v 2 Shelomoh¦158451 \add ≈replied\add* to Hiram,
\v 3 “You yourself knew David my father, \add ≡and you knew\add* that he wasn't able to build a temple for his god Yahweh his god because of the \add ≈constant state\add* of warfare until Yahweh \add ≈would allow us to defeat all our enemies\add*.
\v 4 \add ≈But¦158488\add* now, my god Yahweh has \add ≈enabled us to have peace¦158496\add* from all around¦158502. \add ≈We have no enemy, and there's no threat of¦158501 danger\add*.
\v 5 \add ≈So\add* listen, I'm \add ≈planning\add* to build a residence for my god Yahweh, just as Yahweh spoke to my father David, ‘Your son who I'll put in your place on your throne, he'll \add be the one to\add* build the house \add ≈to honour me\add*.’\x + \xo 5:5: \xt 2Sam 7:12-13; 1Ch 17:11-12.\x*
\v 6 \add ≈So\add* now, \add please give the\add* command to fell cedars for¦158531 me from the Lebanon \add forest\add*. Let my servants \add ≈accompany\add* your servants and¦158532 I'll \add ≈pay\add* your servants' wages \add ≈whatever amount you set\add*, because you yourself know that there are no \add ≈other workers who know\add* how to fell trees like the Tsidonians.”
\p
\v 7 \add ≈When Hiram got Shelomoh's¦158545 message, he was very happy\add* and said, “Blessed be Yahweh today, who has given David a wise son \add to rule\add* over that great \add ≈nation\add*.”
\v 8 \add *He\add* sent \add this message back\add* to Shelomoh, “I've \add ≈received the¦158563 message\add* that you sent me and I myself will \add ≈supervise everything you want including¦158563\add* cedar and cypress \add ≈logs\add*.
\v 9 My servants will \add ≈transport them down from the forest\add* to the ocean, and I'll \add ≈get them tied into\add* rafts \add ≈and float them to the place you specify and disassemble the rafts\add*. \add ≈Then it'll be up to you to get them where you need them\add*. \add ≈All I ask for, is food for my family and workers\add*.”
\p
\v 10 \add ≈So\add* Hiram \add ≈supplied\add* all¦158601 the cedar and¦158606 cypress \add ≈logs\add* that Shelomoh¦158599 \add ≈ordered\add*
\v 11 and each year Shelomoh \add ≈supplied\add* Hiram with¦158625 \add ≈three thousand tonnes\add* of wheat and \add ≈four hundred thousand litres of pure\add* \add olive\add* oil.
\v 12 Yahweh gave Shelomoh wisdom just like \add ≈he'd said he would\add*, and there was¦158635 peace between Hiram and Shelomoh and the two of them \add ≈made a treaty with each other\add*.
\p
\v 13 King Shelomoh \add ≈forced thirty thousand men from¦158645 all over Yisrael to become his workers\add*,
\v 14 and each month he sent \add ≈a group of\add* ten thousand of them to \add work in\add* Lebanon. \add @Each group\add* \add ≈would spend\add* a month in Lebanon and then two months \add ≈at home\add* \add again\add*. Adoniram was \add ≈put in charge of all that\add*.\x + \xo 5:14: \xt 1Ki 12:18.\x*
\v 15 Shelomoh¦158701 \add also\add* \add ≈forced eighty thousand men to cut stones in¦158708 the hill country and¦158691 seventy thousand men to haul loads\add*.
\v 16 Then¦158725 besides Shelomoh's¦158722 chief officers who were \add ≈in charge of\add* the work, there were \add another\add* three thousand three hundred supervisors of the workers.
\v 17 The king commanded \add ≈them to quarry large, valuable blocks of dressed stone for the temple foundations\add*.
\v 18 Shelomoh's and Hiram's workers and the Gebalites shaped the stones and prepared the timber for building the temple.
\c 6
\s1 Shelomoh begins temple construction
\rem /s1 Solomon Builds the Temple
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add So it was that\add* four¦159065 hundred¦159066 and¦159065 eighty¦159063 years after¦159068 the Israelis¦159071 left¦159068 Egypt¦159074, in¦159063 the second¦159081 month of the fourth¦159076 year of Shelomoh's¦159083 reign¦159082 over¦159084 Yisrael¦159071, he¦159087 began the construction of Yahweh's¦159089 residence.
\v 2 \add ≈It would be\add* twenty-seven¦159101 metres long¦159100, nine metres wide¦159102, and fourteen metres high¦159105.\f + \fr 6:2 \ft About the height of a modern five-storey building (and the same height as Noah's barge).\f*
\v 3 \add ≈The five-metre wide porch¦159107 on¦159122 the front¦159110 was the same length¦159115 as the temple¦159111 width, so nine metres¦159122 long\add*.
\v 4 The window¦159131 \add openings\add* narrowed \add towards the outer edge of the wall\add*.
\v 5 \add ≈Against¦159143 the outside of the back wall and¦159135 the two sides\add*, he built¦159135 \add three-storey (but lower than the wall)\add* side rooms.
\v 6 \add ≈The lowest¦159156 level was just over two metres¦159158 wide, the middle¦159160 level was half a metre wider, and¦159164 the top level the same again\add* \add (because the temple wall got thinner as it went up)\add*. \add ≈The temple walls had ledges¦159169 built into them for¦159171 the beams to sit on¦159158, so they wouldn't have¦159175 to go through\add* the walls.
\p
\v 7 While the temple¦159196 was being built, all of the \add huge\add* stones¦159182 were dressed at the quarry¦159185 \add site\add*, so there was no noise from any hammer¦159187 or axe¦159188 or iron¦159192 tool \add ≈at the construction site\add*.
\p
\v 8 The \add ≈entrance¦159199 to the bottom level of siderooms was on¦159207 the south¦159206 side¦159204 of the temple¦159205, then from there, stairs went¦159208 up¦159208 to the middle¦159211 level, and¦159212 more stairs to the top level\add*.
\v 9 \add ≈So¦159219\add* he¦159219 built¦159219 the¦159222 temple \add walls\add*, then¦159219 \add ≈it¦159223 was roofed with¦159230 cedar¦159230 beams¦159228 and¦159219 planks¦159229\add*,
\v 10 and he¦159232 built¦159232 the \add ≈siderooms—each level just over two metres¦159243 high¦159244 and attached\add* to the temple with¦159249 cedar¦159250 \add beams\add*.
\p
\v 11 Then Yahweh¦159256 \add ≈had this message¦159254 sent\add* to Shelomoh¦159259,
\v 12 “This¦159263 residence that you're¦159266 building¦159267, if¦159268 you \add ≈comply with¦159271 my regulations¦159271 and follow my judgements¦159274 and obey\add* all¦159279 my commands¦159281, then I'll¦159294 \add ≈ensure that everything I promised¦159287 your¦159294 father¦159294 David¦159293 will¦159284 come to¦159291 pass\add*.
\v 13 I will live among \add you\add* Israelis¦159299 and¦159296 I'll¦159301 never abandon¦159301 my people Yisrael¦159299.”
\s1 The temple furnishings
\rem /s1 The Temple's Interior; The Interior Furnishings of the Temple
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 3:8-14)
\p
\v 14 \add ≈So\add* Shelomoh¦159309 finished¦159313 the construction of the temple¦159312,
\v 15 and on¦159321 the inside he made walls of¦159318 cedar¦159322 boards. The¦159323 floor was made with¦159321 cypress¦159338 boards, and the walls were panelled from¦159331 the floor to the ceiling¦159328.
\v 16 Inside¦159358 the temple, nine metres at¦159348 the rear \add were closed off\add* to make an¦159359 inner¦159359 room¦159359 called ‘the very holy¦159360 place¦159361’. That too had walls¦159355 made of¦159360 cedar¦159349 boards¦159348.\x + \xo 6:16: \xt Exo 26:33-34.\x*
\v 17 That¦159367 left the¦159368 main temple¦159368 room as eighteen metres¦159364 long,
\v 18 and it had cedar boards on the walls \add decorated with\add* carvings¦159376 of buds and flowers \add ≈in blossom¦159379\add*. \add ≈All the internal walls were covered with cedar so none of the large stones¦159383 were visible¦159384\add*.
\p
\v 19 The¦159387 inner¦159386 room¦159386 at the back was to¦159392 house¦159389 the box¦159396 containing Yahweh's¦159398 agreement¦159397,
\v 20 and the room¦159401 (nine metres long and wide and high) was overlaid with pure¦159413 gold¦159413 and equipped with an \add incense\add* altar¦159415 overlaid with cedar¦159416.
\v 21 Then¦159418 Shelomoh¦159419 overlaid the \add ≈main room\add* with pure¦159425 gold, and¦159418 he¦159426 \add hung\add* gold chains¦159427 crossing across¦159426 the entrance to the inner¦159431 room that was overlaid with gold.
\v 22 He overlaid¦159440 \add the interior of\add* the entire¦159437 temple with gold until¦159442 it was¦159444 all finished¦159444, including¦159448 overlaying the altar¦159450 belonging¦159453 to the inner¦159453 room¦159453 with gold.\x + \xo 6:22: \xt Exo 30:1-3.\x*
\rem /s1 The Furnishings of the Temple
\p
\v 23 In the inner¦159458 room¦159458, he¦159457 made two¦159459 winged creatures that were five metres¦159465 high¦159466 and¦159457 made of olive¦159463 wood.\x + \xo 6:23-28: \xt Exo 25:18-20.\x*
\v 24 Each wing was over two metres long, \add ≈giving each one a total wingspan\add* of four and¦159468 a half metres.
\v 25 \add ≈Both creatures were the same size and¦159493 shape¦159493\add*,
\v 26 \add and\add* \add ≈they were both five metres¦159502 high\add*.
\v 27 The \add two\add* winged creatures \add ≈were placed across the inner¦159514 room where their wingtips touched each other in¦159524 the centre and¦159507 their outer wings touched the walls\add*.
\v 28 \add Both of\add* the¦159545 \add ≈wooden carvings\add* were overlaid¦159542 with gold¦159546.
\p
\v 29 He \add had\add* all¦159549 the \add interior\add* walls¦159551 of¦159551 the temple engraved¦159556 with winged creatures and palm¦159559 trees¦159559 and flowers \add ≈in bloom\add*,
\v 30 and¦159565 he¦159569 overlaid¦159569 the¦159567 floor¦159567 \add ≈of¦159567 both rooms\add* with gold¦159570.
\p
\v 31 He¦159577 made¦159577 doors¦159578 and¦159574 doorposts¦159583 for the¦159582 inner¦159576 room¦159576 with olive¦159581 wood,
\v 32 and \add ≈both¦159586\add* doors¦159587 were \add ≈decorated\add* with carvings of¦159593 winged creatures and palm¦159595 trees¦159595 and flowers \add ≈in bloom\add*, then¦159586 they were overlaid¦159598 with gold¦159599 that was hammered¦159600 over the carvings.
\v 33 He¦159612 made¦159612 the doorposts¦159615 for the main temple¦159614 entrance¦159613 with¦159619 olive¦159618 wood,
\v 34 and¦159622 the two folding doors¦159623 themselves each had two folding sections made of cypress¦159625.
\v 35 \add These doors were also decorated with\add* carvings of winged creatures and palm¦159639 trees¦159639 and flowers \add ≈in bloom\add*, and then¦159637 overlaid¦159642 with gold¦159643 smoothed¦159644 down over the¦159647 carvings.
\p
\v 36 He¦159650 built¦159649 the¦159652 inner¦159653 courtyard¦159652 with three¦159654 rows¦159655 of dressed stones and¦159657 a row¦159657 of cedar¦159659 beams¦159658.
\p
\v 37 The foundation¦159663 for Yahweh's¦159665 residence was laid in¦159666 the second month¦159666 of the fourth¦159662 year¦159661 of Shelomoh's reign,
\v 38 and it was completed¦159677 in¦159669 the eighth¦159676 month of his eleventh year¦159669—meeting all his specifications¦159684 and requirements. So¦159673 it took \add over\add* seven¦159687 years¦159688 to build¦159686 it.
\c 7
\s1 Shelomoh's palace
\rem /s1 Solomon's Palace; Solomon's Palace and Other Buildings; Solomon Builds His Palace
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Shelomoh¦159694 \add also\add* spent thirteen years¦159697 getting his¦159692 own¦159692 palace finished¦159698.
\v 2 He¦159705 built¦159705 the ‘Lebanon¦159711 Forest¦159710 Hall’ that was forty-six metres long¦159714, twenty-three metres wide¦159717, and fourteen metres high¦159720, with four¦159722 rows¦159723 of cedar pillars and cedar beams¦159726 sitting across the pillars.
\v 3 It¦159732 was¦159732 roofed¦159732 with¦159733 cedar¦159733 boards on¦159733 forty-five¦159742 supports set out into three rows of fifteen.
\v 4 There were three rows¦159750 of window gaps arranged in sets of three.
\v 5 All¦159758 the¦159760 entrances and doorposts¦159761 were made \add with wood\add* \add ≈planed with square¦159762 sides\add*, and they were arranged in sets of three¦159769.
\p
\v 6 He made¦159775 \add &his\add* covered porch with pillars. It was twenty-three metres long¦159778 and¦159772 fourteen metres wide¦159781.
\p
\v 7 He¦159795 made¦159800 a \add ≈hall\add* for \add &his\add* throne where¦159794 he¦159800 would judge¦159795 \add >cases\add*—\add called\add* ‘The Judgement¦159799 Hall’. It¦159801 was¦159801 covered¦159801 with¦159802 cedar¦159802 from¦159803 floor¦159803 to¦159804 ceiling.
\p
\v 8 His¦159824 actual residence \add ≈behind\add* the hall was constructed¦159817 in a similar¦159817 way. Shelomoh¦159827 also built a house¦159820 for¦159822 his wife who was Far-oh's daughter¦159822.\x + \xo 7:8: \xt 1Ki 3:1.\x*
\p
\v 9 All these¦159833 buildings were¦159834 constructed with expensive stones¦159834 that had been cut to size and trimmed with saws inside¦159840 and out. They went from the foundation¦159842 up to the eaves, and from the outside¦159846 across to the great¦159850 courtyard¦159849.
\v 10 These large¦159856, costly¦159854 stones in the foundations¦159852 \add were cut\add* \add ≈in two sizes, with the largest¦159856 almost five metres¦159859\add* \add long\add*.
\v 11 On top of those were¦159864 other costly¦159866 stones¦159865 \add ≈of various sizes\add*, and¦159869 then¦159864 cedar¦159869 \add beams\add*.
\v 12 The¦159883 largest courtyard had three¦159874 rows¦159875 of dressed stones and one row¦159877 of cedar¦159879 beams¦159878, as did the inner¦159884 temple courtyard and the \add ≈palace hall\add*.
\s1 Huram comes to work
\rem /s1 Products of Hiram the Bronzeworker; Furnishings for the Temple; Huram's Task; The Temple's Furnishings
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 13 King Shelomoh¦159891 sent¦159889 for \add a craftsman\add* in Tsor \add !(Tyre) called\add* Huram.\f + \fr 7:13 \ft Sometimes (including this passage) his named is spelt ‘Hiram’, but we've opted to consistently name him ‘Huram’ (as per 2 Chr. 4:11) to conveniently avoid confusion with King Hiram (also from Tsor/Tyre).\f*
\v 14 His mother was a widow¦159901 with Naftali¦159904 ancestry, and his¦159905 father¦159905 had been a bronze engraver in Tsor. He¦159911 was \add ≈very knowledgeable¦159920 and skilled¦159917 in everything to do¦159921\add* with bronze, and \add so\add* he¦159926 came¦159926 to work¦159924 for King¦159929 Shelomoh¦159930.
\s1 The two bronze pillars
\rem /s1 The Two Bronze Columns
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 3:15-17)
\p
\v 15 He¦159938 cast two \add hollow\add* bronze¦159943 pillars¦159942—each being just over eight¦159944 metres tall and¦159938 measuring over five metres around.
\v 16 Then¦159961 he made¦159963 two decorative bronze¦159970 caps for those pillars¦159968—each over two metres¦159972 high.
\v 17 To¦159988 hang over them, he made ornamental chains¦159987 and¦159997 wreaths¦159985—seven sets over the cap on each pillar¦159993.
\v 18 Then¦160001 he¦160001 made¦160001 two rows¦160006 of \add decorative bronze\add* pomegranates¦160020 to¦160012 go around¦160007 each of the two caps.
\v 19 The¦160026 two caps were each shaped like lilies that¦160027 were nearly two metres¦160036 high
\v 20 and¦160038 there were¦160039 two hundred¦160053 \add bronze\add* pomegranates¦160052 in rows¦160054 around¦160055 each cap. NEED A DIAGRAM
\p
\v 21 \add @Huram\add* \add ≈stood\add* the \add two\add* pillars¦160063 at the temple¦160065 entrance porch¦160064. (The left¦160080 pillar¦160069 got \add ≈nicknamed\add* ‘Yakin¦160075’ and¦160060 the one on the right¦160070, ‘Boaz¦160085’.)
\v 22 The two bronze caps shaped like lilies \add were placed on\add* top¦160088 of¦160088 the pillars¦160089 and¦160092 so the pillar construction was¦160092 completed¦160092.
\s1 The bronze water tank
\rem /s1 The Bronze Tank
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 4:2-5)
\p
\v 23 \add @Huram\add* also cast a very large round basin \add nicknamed\add* ‘The sea¦160099’ that was five¦160110 metres across (so a circumference of fourteen metres) and¦160096 over two metres high¦160112.
\v 24 \add ≈Part of the same casting was two¦160135 rows¦160136 of decorative buds that went¦160126 around¦160125 it¦160127 under¦160122 the lip¦160123 of the basin. There were¦160138 about¦160123 eighteen buds for each metre of circumference\add*.
\v 25 \add ≈Twelve bronze cattle had also been cast and the large basin was sitting on their¦160167 backs. The cattle were facing outwards with three facing towards¦160168 each of the four compass points\add*.
\v 26 \add ≈The basin was about¦160170 8cm thick¦160170 and¦160170 it¦160181 had a lip¦160174 around the outside like a lily¦160178 blossom¦160177. It could contain¦160181 around forty-four thousand¦160179 litres\add* \add of water\add*.
\s1 The bronze basin holders
\rem /s1 The Bronze Carts
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 27 Then¦160185 \add @Huram\add* cast ten¦160188 bronze¦160189 carts \add for individual water basins\add*. Each one was nearly two metres long and two metres wide¦160197 and just over a metre tall,
\v 28 and¦160207 they had frames on the sides with panels between¦160208 them¦160206.
\v 29 There were bronze lions¦160218, oxen, and winged creatures \add in relief\add* on¦160222 the panels, and above¦160226 and below¦160227 those, wreaths¦160230 were inset into the bronze.
\v 30 Each cart had two bronze axles¦160239 with four bronze wheels¦160235 attached to them. At each of the top four corners¦160242, there were bronze supports¦160243 to hold a \add wash\add* basin¦160246, and¦160234 these were also cast with \add decorative\add* wreaths¦160251.
\v 31 Inside¦160254 \add ≈those supports there was a round¦160259 frame that protruded half¦160264 a metre upwards¦160256 and¦160253 was inset by a quarter of a metre. It also¦160266 had engravings on¦160257 it inside¦160254 square¦160273 frames\add*.
\v 32 The axles¦160281 were part of the main casting, and¦160277 the four¦160277 70cm \add bronze\add* wheels¦160285 were below the panels.
\v 33 The¦160291 design of¦160291 the¦160292 wheels was¦160293 similar to chariot¦160295 wheels, \add ≈but¦160291\add* with their axles¦160296, rims¦160297, spokes¦160298, and¦160291 hubs¦160299 all cast \add from bronze\add*.
\v 34 Each cart had four handles as part of¦160307 the casting—one projecting from¦160310 each corner¦160307.
\v 35 There was¦160315 a 25cm \add bronze\add* band around¦160322 the top¦160315 of each cart as well as supports and¦160315 frames¦160327—all part of the main casting.
\v 36 He¦160330 engraved winged creatures, lions¦160339, and palm¦160340 trees¦160340 on the flat areas of the carts wherever there was space¦160341, and \add ≡engraved\add* wreaths¦160344 around¦160345 them.
\v 37 So¦160349 that's how he¦160348 made¦160348 the ten¦160350 carts—all the same shape¦160356 and size using the same mold.
\p
\v 38 \add @Huram\add* also cast ten bronze¦160364 \add wash\add* basins for the ten carts—each one was almost two metres¦160372 across and could hold 900 litres \add of water\add*.\x + \xo 7:38: \xt Exo 30:17-21.\x*
\v 39 He¦160384 put five¦160388 \add basins on\add* carts on each side¦160391 of the temple (north¦160399 and south \add of the east-facing temple\add*), and he¦160403 put the huge basin \add on its bronze cattle\add* near the south-east corner¦160408.
\rem /s1 Summary List of Temple Furnishings
\rem /r (2 Chronicles 4.11—5.1)
\v 40 Huram \add also\add* made¦160412 the basins and the \add ash\add* shovels¦160419 and the bowls¦160422.
\s1 The list of temple objects
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 4:11–5:1)
\p \add ≈So\add* Huram finished¦160423 everything that¦160431 King¦160433 Shelomoh¦160434 had requested him to make for¦160433 Yahweh's¦160436 residence:
\v 41 two¦160439 \add massive\add* pillars¦160447 with \add large\add* decorated caps and¦160449 then networks¦160449 of chain over the caps,
\v 42 the¦160465 four¦160466 hundred pomegranates¦160473 with each pillar¦160486 having two strings of one¦160475 hundred of them placed over them,
\v 43 the ten carts and¦160488 the ten basins¦160494 on¦160496 them,
\v 44 the \add huge\add* water tank and¦160504 the twelve cattle supporting it,
\v 45 and the pots¦160515 and shovels¦160518 and bowls¦160521 and all¦160523 the other containers.
\p All the¦160525 above was made¦160530 from polished¦160537 bronze¦160536 by Huram for King¦160532 Shelomoh¦160533 for Yahweh's¦160535 residence.
\v 46 The king¦160542 \add had them\add* cast in the clay in the Yordan¦160540 valley¦160539 between¦160545 Sukkot¦160546 and¦160547 Tsaretan.
\v 47 \add ≈None¦160551 of¦160554 the above was weighed¦160562, because¦160557 bronze¦160563 was so plentiful that it¦160561 didn't¦160560 matter\add*.
\p
\v 48 Shelomoh¦160566 also had the gold items made¦160565 for Yahweh's¦160573 residence, including¦160577 the gold \add incense\add* altar¦160575 and¦160565 the gold table¦160579 for the \add ≈sacred\add* bread¦160582,\x + \xo 7:48: a \xt Exo 30:1-3; \xo b \xt Exo 25:23-30.\x*
