\r (2 Chr. 8:3-18)
\p
\v 15 Now¦162384 this¦162364 is¦162365 the matter of the forced¦162367 labour¦162367 that¦162368 King¦162372 Shelomoh¦162373 brought up to¦162374 build¦162374 Yahweh's¦162378 residence and his¦162381 \add ≈palace\add*, the \add ≈raised¦162370 terraces\add* and the Yerushalem¦162387 \add city\add* wall¦162386, and \add the cities of\add* Hatsor¦162390, Megiddo¦162393 and Gezer¦162396.
\v 16 (Egypt's¦162401 King¦162399 Far-oh had seized Gezer¦162406 and set fire¦162408 to it and killed¦162414 the Canaanites¦162411 who¦162412 had lived¦162412 in¦162408 the city¦162413. Then he'd given¦162415 it as a \add ≈wedding present\add* to his¦162417 daughter¦162417 \add ≈when Shelomoh¦162419 had married her\add*.)
\v 17 Shelomoh¦162422 \add ≈rebuilt\add* Gezer¦162425 and¦162426 lower¦162430 Beyt¦162428-Horon¦162429,
\v 18 Baalat, Tadmor¦162437 in¦162439 the¦162440 Yehudah wilderness¦162439,
\v 19 all¦162443 of Shelomoh's¦162449 storehouse¦162446 cities, and the cities for his chariots¦162452 and horses¦162455. They \add also\add* built¦162462 whatever else Shelomoh wanted—in Yerushalem¦162463, in Lebanon¦162464, and in \add ≈the entire¦162443 area\add* of his dominion¦162467.
\v 20 All the \add ≈remaining¦162472\add* Amorite¦162475, Hittite¦162476, Perizzite¦162477, Hivite¦162478, and¦162479 Yebusite¦162479 people¦162471 (they¦162485 weren't descendants of Yisrael¦162484),
\v 21 had¦162495 descendants who¦162488 continued living in Yisrael¦162497. These were people¦162496 groups that¦162506 the¦162491 Israelis¦162497 hadn't been¦162495 able¦162495 to annihilate so Shelomoh¦162500 used them for forced¦162501 labour¦162501 (\add where they remain\add* to this¦162506 day¦162505).
\v 22 \add ≈But¦162508\add* \add *he¦162509\add* didn't¦162510 \add ≈consign any¦162508 Israelis¦162509 to slavehood¦162514\add*, because¦162515 they¦162517 were¦162518 \add ≈his warriors¦162518\add* and servants¦162520, and his officers¦162522 and captains¦162522, and the commanders¦162523 of his chariots¦162524 and horsemen¦162525.
\v 23 \add ≈Some¦162536\add* were¦162530 commanders of¦162530 the five¦162538 hundred¦162539 and¦162538 fifty¦162537 \add ≈work supervisors\add*.
\p
\v 24 Once Far-oh's daughter¦162546 moved out of the City¦162550 of David¦162551 to¦162552 \add ≈the palace\add* that¦162555 he'd built for¦162558 her¦162554, then¦162559 he built the raised terraces.
\p
\v 25 Shelomoh¦162566 offered¦162565 burnt¦162570 offerings and peace¦162571 offerings three¦162567 times¦162568 that year¦162569 on¦162569 the altar¦162574 that he'd built¦162576 for¦162577 Yahweh, and he burnt incense¦162578 to Yahweh with¦162569 it, and he completed¦162584 the \add ≈temple\add*.\x + \xo 9:25: \xt Exo 23:17; 34:23; Deu 16:16.\x*
\rem /s1 Solomon's Commercial Activity
\p
\v 26 King¦162591 Shelomoh¦162592 constructed a fleet¦162589 \add of ships\add* in Ezion¦162593-Geber, which¦162596 is¦162597 near Eylot¦162599 on¦162593 the shore¦162602 of the Red Sea¦162603 in the Edom¦162607 \add ≈region¦162606\add*.
\v 27 \add King\add* Hiram¦162610 sent¦162609 \add some of\add* his¦162614 servants \add ≈who were experienced sailors to serve with¦162611 Shelomoh's¦162621 men¦162615\add*.
\v 28 They¦162623 \add ≈sailed\add* to Ofir¦162624 and¦162623 brought¦162633 back fourteen tonnes of gold¦162627 that they took¦162625 to Shelomoh¦162637.
\c 10
\s1 The queen from Sheva visits Shelomoh
\rem /s1 The Queen of Sheba Visits Solomon; The Queen of Sheba's Visit; The Visit of the Queen of Sheba; Visit of the Queen of Sheba
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 9:1-12)
\p
\v 1 Now the queen¦162640 from Sheva¦162642 had heard¦162643 a report¦162646 \add ≈about¦162640 how Yahweh¦162649 had blessed\add* Shelomoh¦162647, and she¦162650 came¦162650 to test¦162651 him¦162651 with¦162652 difficult¦162652 questions¦162652.\x + \xo 10:1-10: \xt Mat 12:42; Luk 11:31.\x*
\v 2 She¦162654 came¦162654 to Yerushalem¦162655 with¦162656 \add ≈lots of her top people\add*, camels¦162659 carrying¦162660 spices¦162661 and gemstones and a lot of gold¦162662. Then¦162654 she¦162668 went¦162654 to Shelomoh¦162671 and \add ≈started asking him¦162673 everything that¦162660 had been on¦162656 her mind¦162681\add*.
\v 3 \add *He¦162686\add* \add ≈answered¦162683 all¦162689 her¦162691 questions¦162691—there wasn't any topic that¦162700 he¦162702 wasn't able to¦162703 shed light on\add*.
\v 4 \add ≈When¦162705\add* the queen¦162706 from Sheva¦162708 saw¦162705 \add ≈the extent\add* of¦162712 Shelomoh's¦162713 wisdom¦162712, the \add ≈palace\add* that¦162715 he'd built¦162716,
\v 5 the food¦162718 on¦162735 his table¦162719, the seating¦162720 of his servants¦162723, the uniforms and the service of his waiters, and the burnt¦162727 offerings¦162727 that¦162728 he \add ≈sacrificed\add* at¦162735 Yahweh's¦162731 \add ≈temple\add*, \add ≈she was¦162734 totally lost for words\add*.
\p
\v 6 She¦162739 told the king¦162742, “\add Wow,\add* everything that¦162746 I¦162747 heard¦162747 about you \add back\add* in¦162748 my¦162748 own¦162748 country, and¦162739 about how wise you are, is true.
\v 7 I hadn't believed¦162758 it until¦162760 I \add ≈got here\add* and saw¦162764 it \add ≈with my¦162765 own¦162765 eyes¦162765, but¦162756 then¦162756 I find that¦162779 what I heard is only \+em half\+em* of¦162765 how incredible it is\add*. Your wisdom¦162774 and your goodness is \add way\add* more than what I'd heard \add ≈described\add*.
\v 8 Your¦162791 men¦162783 \add ≈are so fortunate\add* and \add especially\add* your servants¦162785 who are always standing¦162787 in front¦162788 of you and able to hear¦162790 \add ≈the wise things that you say\add*.
\v 9 May your¦162797 god¦162797 Yahweh be¦162795 blessed¦162798, who¦162799 \add ≈was pleased with¦162801\add* you \add and\add* placed you on¦162801 Yisrael's¦162806 throne¦162805. He¦162813 \add ≈appointed¦162813\add* you as king¦162814 to¦162814 \add ≈rule them fairly and honestly\add* because¦162807 Yahweh loves¦162807 Yisrael forever.”
\p
\v 10 Then¦162819 \add @the queen\add* gave to¦162820 king¦162820 four tonnes of¦162824 gold¦162825 and very¦162827 many spices and precious¦162830 \add #gemstones\add*. \add ≈Never¦162843 again¦162836 did King¦162820 Shelomoh¦162845 receive more spices than what the queen gave him\add* \add that day\add*.
\p
\v 11 \add King\add* Hiram's¦162849 fleet also¦162847 \add ≈brought¦162855\add* gold¦162853 from Ofir¦162854 \add as well as\add* a lot of almug¦162858 wood and¦162861 precious¦162862 \add #gemstones\add*.
\v 12 The king¦162865 used the almug timber to make¦162864 \add ≈railings\add*\f + \fr 10:12 \ft The exact meaning of this Hebrew word is unknown, especially as it's only used once. (Other possibilities for the use of this obviously high-quality timber includes ‘steps’ or ‘pillars’.)\f* for \add ≈the temple\add* and \add ≈the palace\add*, and lyres¦162876 and harps¦162877 for the \add ≈musicians\add*. Such \add ≈a quantity and quality of almug wood has¦162886 never again\add* been¦162886 brought in or seen¦162886 \add in Israel\add* to this day¦162888.
\p
\v 13 Then King¦162891 Solomon¦162892 gave¦162893 the queen¦162894 from Sheva¦162896 \add ≈everything she wanted\add*—whatever she requested \add ≈on top of\add* what he \add ≈had¦162906 already given¦162906\add* to her. Then she departed with her servants¦162916 and¦162891 returned to her country.
\s1 Shelomoh's wealth
\rem /s1 Solomon's Wealth and Splendour; King Solomon's Wealth; Solomon's Splendour
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 9:13-29)
\p
\v 14 The¦162920 \add ≈amount\add* of¦162920 gold¦162921 that¦162922 came¦162924 to¦162925 Shelomoh¦162925 in¦162926 one¦162927 year¦162926 was¦162919 \add around\add* twenty-two tonnes,
\v 15 \add ≈not counting what came from¦162936 explorers and traders¦162937\add*, and all¦162940 the Arab¦162943 kings¦162942, and the governors¦162944 of¦162944 the land¦162945.
\p
\v 16 King¦162948 Shelomoh¦162949 had two¦162950 hundred¦162950 body¦162951 shields made¦162947 from beaten¦162953 gold \add overlaid over wood\add*—each shield took six¦162954 kilograms of gold.
\v 17 \add He also had\add* three hundred¦162966 \add smaller\add* shields¦162967 made—each of them¦162978 \add covered\add* with two kilograms of gold—and¦162964 placed in \add &his\add* ‘Lebanon¦162982 Forest¦162981 Hall’.
\p
\v 18 The¦162986 king¦162986 had a large¦162990 ivory¦162989 throne¦162987 made¦162985 and¦162991 overlaid¦162991 with pure gold¦162992.
\v 19 \add *It\add* had six steps¦162996 \add ≈going up to\add* \add *it¦163002\add* and \add ≈the seat-back was rounded¦163000 at the top¦162998\add*. \add ≈It had armrests on both¦163010 sides, then a lion on each side next to the armrests\add*,
\v 20 plus¦163026 twelve lions¦163018 standing¦163019 one on¦163021 each side of the six¦163023 steps¦163024. \add ≈There was nothing like it¦163029 in any¦163031 other kingdom¦163033.\add*
\p
\v 21 All¦163035 of Shelomoh's cups were gold, and¦163035 the \add ≈various dishes\add* in¦163053 the ‘Lebanon¦163046 Forest¦163045 Hall’ were \add also\add* pure¦163048 gold. \add ≈Nothing was made out of silver¦163050 because it¦163052 was considered to be of little value during Shelomoh's time¦163053\add*
\v 22 because¦163057 the king¦163060 had a fleet of ships that joined \add King\add* Hiram's¦163064 fleet. Every three¦163066 years¦163067 the fleet would return bringing gold¦163073 and silver¦163074, ivory, monkeys¦163076 and baboons.\f + \fr 10:22 \ft baboons: We're not certain of the meaning of this last word (some suggest ‘peacocks’), nor are we sure of the type of the ships named ‘Tarshish ships’.\f*
\p
\v 23 King¦163080 Shelomoh¦163081 became¦163079 \add ≈richer¦163085 and¦163079 wiser\add* than \add ≈any other king¦163080 in the world\add*,
\v 24 and¦163088 \add even\add* \add ≈people from distant countries would come to see\add* Shelomoh¦163095 to listen¦163096 to the wisdom¦163099 that¦163100 God¦163103 had¦163102 given \add ≈him\add*.
\v 25 Every \add ≈visitor\add* would bring¦163107 a gift: gold¦163113 containers, clothes¦163114, weapons¦163115, spices¦163116, horses¦163117 and¦163106 mules¦163118. \add ≈This continued year after year\add*.
\p
\v 26 Shelomoh¦163126 \add ≈acquired\add* 1,400 chariots¦163127 and twelve thousand¦163140 horsemen¦163128. He \add ≈stationed¦163142\add* \add most of them\add* in his chariot¦163144 cities¦163143 and \add some\add* with¦163143 \add *him\add* \add there\add* in Yerushalem¦163148.\x + \xo 10:26: \xt 1Ki 4:26.\x*
\v 27 \add While he was\add* king¦163151, silver¦163154 in Yerushalem¦163155 \add ≈was given a value similar to\add* stones¦163156, and¦163150 cedar¦163158 timber \add ≈was given a value like¦163156 that of\add* the sycamores¦163160 that \add ≈grow in plenty\add* in the lowlands.\x + \xo 10:27: \xt Deu 17:17.\x*
\v 28 Shelomoh¦163169 \add ≈acquired\add* horses¦163167 from Egypt¦163170 and¦163171 from Kue—\add ≈his traders¦163172 would acquire them¦163174\add* from Kue for the king¦163173 for a price¦163176.\x + \xo 10:28: \xt Deu 17:16.\x*
\v 29 An Egyptian¦163181 chariot¦163180 went¦163178 for six¦163182 hundred silver¦163184 coins and a horse¦163185 for one¦163187 hundred and fifty¦163186. Then¦163178 \add ≈they'd export them again to¦163189 the¦163191 Hittite¦163192 and Aramean\add* kings¦163193.
\c 11
\s1 Shelomoh turns from God
\rem /s1 Solomon's Many Wives; Solomon's Errors; Solomon Turns Away from God; Solomon's Wives
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Now King¦163199 Shelomoh¦163200 loved¦163201 many foreign¦163203 women—besides Far-oh's daughter¦163207 \add there were\add* Moabite women, Ammonite¦163211 women, Edomite women, Sidonian¦163213 women, and Hittite¦163214 women.\x + \xo 11:1: \xt Neh 13:26; Deu 17:17.\x*
\v 2 \add They were\add* from the¦163218 nations that¦163219 Yahweh¦163222 had¦163220 told Yisrael¦163226 about¦163225, “You must not \add ≈mix with people¦163225 from\add* \add @those nations\add*, and¦163231 they must not \add ≈join together with\add* you. They will \add ≈certainly entice you to worship\add* their¦163242 gods¦163242.” \add However,\add* Shelomoh¦163245 clung to them¦163246 for love¦163246.\x + \xo 11:2: \xt Exo 34:16; Deu 7:3-4.\x*
\v 3 \add ≈He¦163248 had seven¦163253 hundred wives who were kings' daughters, as well as\add* three¦163256 hundred slave-wives, and¦163248 his wives \add ≈were instrumental in his turning¦163258 away\add* \add from Yahweh\add*.
\v 4 By the time¦163265 of his old¦163266 age¦163266, Shelomoh's¦163267 wives¦163268 had \add ≈persuaded him to worship the gods¦163274 from their countries\add*—his heart wasn't \add ≈totally dedicated\add* to Yahweh¦163283 like¦163285 his father¦163287 David¦163286 had been.
\v 5 \add ≈So¦163289\add* Shelomoh¦163290 \add ≈started to worship\add* the Tsidonians' God Ashtoret and¦163295 the Ammonites' detestable¦163297 God Milkom,
\v 6 \add ≈so\add* \add *he\add* \add ≈did many things that Yahweh had said were\add* evil¦163302—he didn't¦163305 remain fully \add ≈committed to\add* Yahweh like¦163309 his¦163310 father¦163310 David¦163309 had been.
\v 7 Then¦163313 Shelomoh¦163315 built¦163314 a place¦163316 to worship Kemosh¦163317, the Moabites detestable God, on¦163320 a hill¦163320 facing Yerushalem¦163325, and¦163326 also for¦163326 Molek¦163326, the Ammonites' detestable God.
\v 8 He¦163332 did¦163332 those things for his¦163335 foreign¦163336 wives¦163335 who were burning incense and¦163338 sacrificing¦163338 to¦163339 their¦163339 gods¦163339.
\p
\v 9 \add ≈Although Yisrael's¦163351 god¦163350 Yahweh had appeared¦163352 twice to¦163353 Shelomoh¦163343, he¦163341 now turned¦163346 his¦163347 devotion away from¦163348 Yahweh, who¦163352 then¦163341 became angry¦163341 with¦163343 him¦163353\add*.
\v 10 \add @Yahweh\add* had¦163356 \add ≈warned\add* \add @Shelomoh\add* not¦163362 to¦163364 follow after¦163365 other¦163367 gods¦163366, but¦163356 he didn't¦163362 \add ≈obey\add* Yahweh's¦163374 \add ≈instructions¦163356\add*,
\v 11 and Yahweh¦163378 told¦163391 \add *him\add*, “\add ≈You have¦163387\add* this¦163384 against you: you haven't kept my agreement¦163388 and \add ≈you've¦163387 disobeyed\add* my statutes¦163389 that¦163390 I \add ≈gave\add* you. \add So\add* I'll¦163394 definitely tear the kingdom¦163397 away from¦163398 you and give¦163399 it¦163399 to¦163400 your¦163400 servant¦163400,
\v 12 except that for the sake¦163407 of your father¦163409 David¦163408 I won't¦163405 do¦163406 it in¦163404 your lifetime¦163404, rather, I'll¦163409 tear¦163412 the kingdom away¦163412 from your son¦163411.
\v 13 \add ≈What's more,\add* I won't¦163420 tear¦163421 the whole¦163417 kingdom¦163419 away—I'll¦163421 leave one¦163423 tribe¦163422 for your¦163425 son¦163425 \add to rule\add* for my servant¦163428 David's¦163427 sake, and¦163429 for the sake of¦163429 my chosen¦163432 city Yerushalem¦163430.”
\s1 Shelomoh's enemies
\rem /s1 Solomon's Enemies; Adversaries of Solomon; Solomon's Adversaries
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 14 Then¦163434 Yahweh¦163435 \add ≈caused\add* Hadad¦163439 the Edomite (and¦163434 a descendant of the Edomite king¦163442) \add ≈to stand\add* against¦163437 Shelomoh¦163437.
\v 15 \add In the past\add* \add ≈when\add* David¦163448 had been in Edom with¦163447 \add &his\add* army¦163455 commander¦163454 Yoav¦163453 to¦163456 bury¦163456 their dead¦163459, they had killed¦163459 every¦163461 male¦163463 in Edom.
\v 16 (Yoav¦163472 and¦163473 \add ≈the Israeli warriors\add* had had to stay there¦163471 for six¦163467 months¦163468 to kill the Edomite males.)
\v 17 \add ≈However,\add* Hadad¦163485 had been a small child at that time, and¦163484 \add *he'd\add* fled¦163484 to Egypt¦163493 with some¦163489 of his¦163490 father's¦163490 Edomite servants¦163489.
\v 18 They'd \add ≈passed through Midyan¦163499 and then¦163498 Paran¦163501 where they'd gained some more men¦163503 before entering Egypt¦163507 where King¦163511 Far-oh gave¦163514 him a house¦163517 and land¦163521, and provided him with food¦163518\add*.
\v 19 Far-oh \add ≈took a liking to\add* Hadad¦163526 and¦163525 gave¦163531 him¦163533 his¦163538 wife¦163534 Queen Tahpenes' sister \add ≈to marry\add*.
\v 20 Tahpenes' sister¦163545 \add ≈gave¦163543 birth¦163543\add* to¦163544 his son Genubath, his son, and¦163543 Tahpenes weaned¦163550 him¦163550 in the midst of the house of Pharaoh. And¦163555 Genubath was¦163555 in the house of Pharaoh in the midst of the sons¦163560 of Pharaoh.
\p
\v 21 \add ≈When¦163563\add* Hadad in¦163565 Egypt¦163565 heard¦163564 that¦163566 \add both\add* David¦163569 and \add &his\add* army¦163579 commander¦163577 Yoav¦163576 had¦163575 died¦163575, Hadad said¦163580 to Pharaoh¦163584, “\add ≈Give me¦163585 permission to leave\add*, and I'll go¦163586 \add back\add* to my¦163589 land¦163589.”
\p
\v 22 \add ≈But\add* Far-oh \add ≈asked\add* him¦163592, “But \add ≈what's¦163595 wrong with¦163599 staying here¦163600? You've stunned me¦163611 by asking\add* to go¦163602 to your¦163605 land¦163605.”
\p “Nothing's \add lacking here\add*,” he¦163606 \add ≈replied\add*, “but¦163591 \add I have to go\add*—\add ≈please give me permission to leave\add*.”
\p
