\p
\v 21 \add ≈At that time,\add* the people¦167190 of Yisrael¦167191 were divided¦167189 \add ≈concerning their leadership\add*—half of \add *them\add* \add ≈supported\add* Ginnat's¦167200 son¦167198 Tivni¦167197 \add ≈as king¦167201\add*, and¦167202 the other half \add ≈supported\add* Omri¦167204,
\v 22 \add ≈but¦167206\add* those \add ≈supporting Omri prevailed¦167206, so Tivni¦167216 was executed\add* and¦167206 Omri \add remained\add* \add ≈in power\add*.
\v 23 Omri¦167234 \add ≈became¦167233 king\add* in¦167241 the thirty-first¦167227 years of King¦167231 Asa's¦167230 \add reign\add* over Yehudah¦167232, and¦167228 reigned¦167242 over Yisrael¦167237 for twelve years—six¦167243 of them from Tirtsah¦167241.
\rem /s1 Samaria the New Capital
\v 24 He bought¦167247 a hill from¦167252 \add a man named\add* Shemer for¦167254 seventy kilograms of silver¦167255, then¦167247 built \add his capital\add* city¦167264 on¦167254 it. He named it Shomron¦167273 \add !(Samaria¦167251)\add* \add ≈in¦167254 honour of Shemer who'd owned\add* the hill.
\p
\v 25 Omri¦167276 did \add ≈what Yahweh¦167279 had said was\add* evil—\add in fact\add* he¦167280 did more evil things than all¦167281 those¦167282 \add kings\add* who¦167282 \add ≈preceded\add* him¦167283.
\v 26 He \add ≈followed (Nabat's son¦167290) Yarave'am's¦167289 behaviour including¦167293\add* causing¦167296 Yisrael to sin¦167296 by provoking¦167300 Yisrael's¦167299 god¦167304 Yahweh¦167303 to get angry with¦167293 their¦167306 idols¦167306.
