\v 37 Then¦170051 he¦170051 found¦170051 \add ≈another¦170053 prophet\add* and said¦170054, “Strike¦170055 me¦170055, please¦170056,” and the man¦170058 struck¦170057 him \add with a sword\add*—wounding¦170060 \add >him\add*.
\v 38 Then¦170062 the \add wounded\add* prophet¦170063 \add ≈disguised¦170069 himself¦170069\add* with¦170070 a covering over his eyes¦170073 and went¦170062 and stood¦170064 on¦170070 the road¦170068 \add to wait\add* for¦170065 the king¦170065.
\v 39 \add ≈When¦170075 the king¦170076 came past\add*, \add @the prophet\add* called out to¦170080 \add *him\add*, saying¦170083, “Your¦170084 servant¦170084 went¦170086 out in the middle of the battle¦170089, and suddenly, a man \add ≈came across with¦170087 a captive\add* and told \add >me¦170095\add*, ‘Guard¦170098 this man. If¦170103 he¦170106 \add ≈goes\add* missing¦170106, your life will¦170107 be \add forfeit\add* in place¦170109 of his¦170110, or¦170111 else you'll¦170115 \add ≈be fined three thousand pieces\add* of silver¦170114.’
\v 40 \add ≈But¦170117 while I was busy with other things, the¦170126 man escaped\add*.”
\p “\add Well,\add* \add ≈by your own testimony, you deserve your punishment\add*,” \add @King¦170126 Ahav\add* \add ≈responded¦170124\add*.
